# Files in this build context that the image does not use.
#
# TWO EFFECTS, AND THE SECOND IS THE REASON THIS FILE EXISTS (#1191, 2026-09-24).
# Docker stops sending them as build context, which is marginal here. And
# `run.sh` excludes them from the build-input hash it stamps each image with, so
# editing the README or `run.sh` itself no longer triggers a rebuild. Before
# this, every edit to either cost one fully-cached rebuild -- about a second and
# no network, so it was wrong only in the safe direction, but it was noise.
#
# EXACT FILENAMES ONLY. `run.sh` reads this file and refuses to interpret
# anything cleverer: a line containing a `*`, a `/`, a `!` or a leading `#`
# beyond these comments makes it fall back to hashing the whole directory. That
# is deliberate -- a half-understood ignore pattern would silently shrink what
# the stamp covers, and an image that is not rebuilt when it should be is the
# bug this whole mechanism exists to prevent. Wrong in the safe direction, both
# times.
#
# So: if you add a pattern here that Docker understands and `run.sh` does not,
# nothing breaks except that you are back to a rebuild per README edit.
README.md
run.sh
devcontainer.json
